Z||000000||Simon Hudson||04-25-2001||02:12 AM||||Well, I’m finally a Fiero owner.  Though it’s not my dream car, at least it’s a start.  I picked up a 1986 Fiero SE from a buddy with only 132,000 km for $2500 Cdn.  IS there anything I should know that might prevent future problems and allow my new baby to run it’s best for as long as she’s able.  What particular maintenance should I do etc?  Thanks for your input.||||reg||10
Z||000001||Cheever2||04-25-2001||09:39 AM||||Same as with any car.  Check your owners maintenance manual. <P>If it ain't broke, don't fix it, unless it's a modification  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   Keep reading this Tech section of the Forum.  For anything you can't do yourself, good luck finding a shop who (A)-doesn't mind working on Fieros, and (B)-ever HAS worked on a Fiero.<P>If you have a question, be sure to click the search button at the top right, then fill in the blanks and click Submit.  I'm frequently amazed how quickly I get exactly what I want from that.  After that, feel free to ask here, that's what it's for.<P>Congratulations on becoming a Fiero owner!  And welcome to the Forum.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000002||FieroJoe||04-25-2001||10:34 AM||||As for particular maintenance that you should be aware of, fix all oil leaks and keep your engine compartment as clean as possible!<P>Other than that, I would just do tune up.  New plugs, wires, distributor cap.  All new filters and an oil change of course, check the tires, basic stuff like that.  Check all fluids and hoses, and you should be good to go.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: FieroJoe87<BR>87 Coupe 5spd<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
